## 1. Introduction
Since the beginning of the 21st century, the consumption of nonrenewable fossil fuels for energy has increased rapidly, and environmental pollution problems such as global warming and harmful gas emissions have become increasingly prominent [1,2]. Therefore, it is necessary to develop and utilize more economical, environmentally friendly and efficient energy storage and conversion technologies to replace traditional fossil fuels [3–6]. Zinc(Zn)- air batteries (ZABs) have received widespread attention due to their environmental friendliness, high cost-effectiveness and practicality [7,8]. The oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) are the basic reactions on the air electrode of Zn-air batteries during discharging and charging processes, respectively [9–11]. However, the electrochemical reaction of air electrodes shows inherently slow dynamics, which leads to the low energy conversion efficiency and poor stability of Zn-air batteries [12–14]. Therefore, it is necessary to design a stable and efficient bifunctional oxygen catalyst to accelerate the reaction kinetics and improve the performance of the battery.
In recent years, an increasing number of bifunctional oxygen catalysts have been developed to improve the electrochemical performance of batteries [15–19]. Noble metal-based nanomaterials such as platinum $(\mathrm{Pt})$ and ruthenium oxides $(\mathsf{R u O}_{2})$ have been considered the most efficient catalysts due to their exceptional catalytic activities. Unfortunately, their high cost seriously impedes their large-scale development [20–23]. Transition metal-based catalysts, such as nickel (Ni), cobalt (Co), and iron (Fe)-based oxides/ sulfides/phosphides/selenides, have gradually become substitutes for noble metal-based catalysts because of their low cost, rich resources, and strong corrosion resistance [24–28]. Among them, transition metal selenides have been considered promising bifunctional OER/ORR catalysts for metal-air batteries due to their good electronic conductivity and high electrochemical oxidationreduction reaction [29–33]. For example, Zheng et al. [30] prepared hollow $\Nu\mathrm{i}_{x}\subset\Lt_{0.85-x}\Lt_{0}$ nanospheres along with enhanced electrochemical performance and long-term stability for the OER (an overpotential of $305~\mathrm{{mV}}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ ), ORR (half-wave potential of 0.78 V) and a peak power density of approximately $100\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ in the application of Zn-air batteries. Sun et al. [32] reported porous $(\mathsf{N i},\mathsf{C o})\mathsf{S e}_{2}$ nanosheets via the direct selenization treatment of $\mathsf{N i C o}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{4}$ nanosheets. The $(\mathsf{N i},\mathsf{C o})\mathsf{S e}_{2}$ nanosheets delivered a low overpotential of $360\mathrm{mV}$ at $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ for OER and a half-wave potential of $0.88\mathrm{V}$ for ORR. The $Z\mathrm{n\cdot}$ -air batteries based on $(\mathsf{N i},\mathsf{C o})\mathsf{S e}_{2}$ nanosheet bifunctional catalysts displayed a peak power density of approximately $110\mathrm{\mw\cm}^{-2}$ Although much research has been done on transition metal selenide catalysts for $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air batteries, a controllable material structure needs to be explored, which provides more active sites and a better electrocatalytic performance for the catalysts.
Metal-organic framework material (MOF)-derived transition metal selenides are a new type of material with a high specific surface area, good chemical stability, and adjustable pore size and composition [33–38]. Zhang et al.[36] used bimetallic CuCoMOFs as templates followed by selenization to prepare coppercobalt selenides encapsulated in N-doped porous carbon (CuCo$\mathsf{S e@N C)}$ with high porosity and specific surface area ( $342\mathrm{~m}^{2}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}.$ ). The assembled $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air batteries, with the use of the modified $\mathsf{C u C o S e@N C}$ bifunctional catalyst, exhibited improved electrochemical performance with a high power density. Therefore, it can be concluded that MOF-derived transition metal selenides can further improve the electrocatalytic performance of Zn-air batteries by combining a carbon protection layer with highly active transition metal selenides. Recently, Zhang et al.[37] designed phosphorus (P)-doped $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ with N-doped carbon as a bifunctional catalyst for Zn-air batteries. The results showed that using
P as a dopant can enhance the catalytic performance of undoped active catalysts, which can be attributed to its large atomic size and electron-donating ability.
Inspired by the abovementioned works, to further study the function and electrocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ in the selfsupporting $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ electrode without the additional binder and conductive carbon black, a phosphorus-doped transition metal selenide/carbon composite catalyst derived from MOF $\left(\mathsf{P-C o S e}_{2}/\mathsf{\Lambda}\right)$ $\mathsf{C@C C})$ was constructed by a self-supporting carbon cloth structure through a simple hydrothermal process with subsequent selenization and phosphatization. As a bifunctional electrocatalyst for OER and ORR, $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ demonstrated exceptional electrocatalytic activities in alkaline electrolytes. More importantly, the $\mathsf{P-C o S e}_{2}/$ ${\mathsf{C@C C}}$ catalyst as the air cathode in ZABs exhibits an excellent power density of $124.4\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ and superior cycling stability without significant decay after working for $^{70\mathrm{~h~}}$ . This work can be extended to other transition metal systems, which provides inspiration for advanced bifunctional oxygen catalysts of ZABs.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Chemicals and reagents
The Cobalt chloride hexahydrate $_\mathrm{CoCl_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ , AR), selenium powder (Se, CP), and zinc acetate dihydrate $(\mathrm{C_{4}H_{6}O_{4}Z n{\cdot}2H_{2}O}$ , AR) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., ltd. The Potassium hydroxide (KOH, $85.0\ \ \%$ , p-phthalic acid $(\mathsf{C}_{8}\mathsf{H}_{6}0_{4}$ $98.5\mathrm{~\}\%)$ , sodium hypophosphite $(\mathsf{N a H}_{2}\mathsf{P O}_{2}{\cdot}\mathsf{H}_{2}0$ , $99.0\mathrm{~\}\%$ , N,Ndimethylformamide (DMF, AR), and methanol were purchased from Tianjin Guangfu Technology Development Co., ltd. The Carbon cloth (CC, W0S1009) was provided by CeTech Co., ltd.
## 2.2. Materials synthesis
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of Co-MOF@CC
First, ${\tt C}_{8}\mathrm{H}_{6}0_{4}$ $\left(0.4980\mathrm{~g},\mathrm{~}3\mathrm{~mmol}\right)$ and ${\mathsf{C o C l}}_{2}{\cdot}6{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ $(0.7138\ \mathrm{g},$ 3 mmol) were dispersed in $40~\mathrm{ml}$ DMF to form a homogeneous, dark blue solution. After mixing at room temperature for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ , $2~\mathrm{ml}$ deionized water was slowly poured into the beaker. Subsequently, the above solution was transferred into a $100~\mathrm{{ml}}$ Teflonlined stainless-steel autoclave with a piece of cleaned carbon cloth $(1\times3\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{2})$ and maintained at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After the autoclave had been cooled to ambient temperature naturally, ${\mathsf{C o-M O F@C C}}$ was produced and cleaned with methanol and DMF several times and dried at $60^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of $C o/C@C C$
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of ${C o S e_{2}}/{C@C C}$
The synthesized ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C@C C}}$ and $50\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{Se}$ powder were separately placed into a porcelain boat. Then, the porcelain boat was placed into a furnace in which Se powder was located upstream and calcined at $500^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ with a heating speed of $3\ {\mathrm{~}^{\circ}}{\mathrm{C~}}{\mathrm{min}}^{-1}$ under an argon atmosphere. Finally, $\cos e_{2}/C@C C$ was obtained after the furnace cooled to room temperature.
