## 2.3. Physical characterization
The crystallographic structure and phase composition of the prepared samples were determined on an X-ray diffraction (XRD, Bruker D8 Advanced) instrument equipped with $\mathrm{Cu-K}\mathfrak{a}$ radiation $(\lambda~=~1.5406~\mathrm{\AA}$ . Field emission scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were obtained for the micromorphological observations on a Hitachi S4800. Elemental mapping analysis was used to collect energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometry (EDS) with transmission electron microscopy (TEM, FEI Tecnai G2 F30). X-ray photoelectron spectrometer (XPS) measurements were gathered on a Thermo Fisher ESCALAB 250 Xi. Thermogravimetric (TG) analysis was performed on a thermogravimetric (NETZSCH STA449F3) analyzer from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathsf C$ with a heating rate of $10^{\circ}\mathsf C$ $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ in air or $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ to characterize the thermal decomposition process. $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms were measured on an Autosorb-iQ station at $77~\mathrm{K}.$ The surface area was calculated with the Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) method, and the pore size distribution was collected using the density functional theory (DFT) method.
## 2.4. Electrochemical measurements
Electrochemical measurements were conducted on a CHI660e electrochemical workstation in a standard three-electrode configuration, along with the prepared specimens, a $\mathrm{Hg/HgO}$ and a Pt plate as the working electrode, the reference electrode, and the counter electrode. The OER activities were examined in $1.0\mathrm{M}\mathrm{KOH}$ , and the ORR activities were completed in 0.1 M KOH, with ${\sf N}_{2}$ saturation and $0_{2}$ saturation, respectively. All potentials were converted by the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) using the equation:
$$
E_{R H E}=E_{H g/H g O}+0.059*p H+0.098
$$
Before determining the polarization curves, cyclic voltammetry (CV) tests were employed to activate the working electrodes at a scan rate of $50\mathrm{\mV}/s$ several times. Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) curves were recorded with a sweep rate of $2~\mathrm{mV}/\varsigma$ , and the data were corrected by $90\%$ iR compensation. The ORR and OER curves were obtained over a range of 1.065–0.165 and 1.124– $1.924\:\mathrm{V}$ . The Tafel plots were obtained according to the formula:
$$
\eta=a+b\log j
$$
where $\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}\boldsymbol{\mathsf{\Pi}}$ , b, and $\mathrm{~j~}$ represent the overpotential, the Tafel slope, and current density, respectively. To measure the electrochemical active surface area (ECSA), the electrochemical double layer capacitance $\left(C_{d l}\right)$ was measured through a CV test with various scan rates (10, 20, 40, 60, 80, and $100\mathrm{mV}/s)$ from 0.999 to 1.099 V (vs RHE). Electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) measurements were evaluated from $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ to $0.1\ \mathrm{Hz}$ at 1.57 V (vs RHE). Stability tests for OER and ORR were displayed in a 1 M KOH solution at a current density of $20\mathrm{\mA\cm}{-2}$ with the chronoamperometry technique and in 0.1 M KOH solution at $_{0.72\mathrm{~V~}}$ with chronopotentiometry technique for 10,000 s, respectively.
A homemade rechargeable $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air battery device was assembled with the prepared catalysts (the cathode), a zinc plate (the anode), a nickel foam (fluid collector), a gas diffusion layer and a 6 M KOH plus 0.2 $\mathsf{M Z n}(\mathsf{O A c})_{2}$ electrolyte. The discharge–charge polarization curve of the zinc air battery was detected on a CHI660E electrochemical workstation from 2.5 to $0.4\mathrm{V}$ at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mV}s^{-1}$ , calculating the power density polarization curve. Subsequently, the stability tests of constant current charge and discharge at different current densities (1, 2, 5, 7.5, and $10\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ ) were obtained on a LAND testing system (Wuhan LAND Electronic Co.
Ltd.). To collect the galvanostatic discharge–charge cycling curves, chronopotentiometry tests were carried out at $5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
Fig. 1 shows the synthetic process for the $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ catalyst through the solvothermal and chemical vapor deposition (CVD) methods. Initially Co-MOF is the precursor that was grown on carbon cloth by the solvothermal method. Then, the Co-MOF grown on carbon cloth was transformed into ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C@C C}}$ through thermal annealing under a $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{Ar}$ atmosphere. The ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C@C C}}$ was then selenized with selenium powder by a simple CVD method to obtain $\cos\mathbf{e}_{2}/\subset@\mathsf{C C}$ , and P atoms were finally incorporated into the material with sodium hypophosphite using the same method.
The structural phase and composition of the resultant samples produced at various stages are first recorded by X-ray diffraction (XRD). As shown in Fig. 2a, the observed diffraction peaks in all specimens in a 2h range of $20{-}30^{\circ}$ are assigned to the CCs. According to the sharpness of the peak with 2h at $8.9^{\circ}$ , $16.0^{\circ}$ , $17.9^{\circ}$ , and $29.2^{\circ}$ , it can be noted that the MOF structure exhibits a good crystalline structure. After high-temperature carbonization, apart from the amorphous carbon enhancing the intensity of the diffraction peak, two distinguishable metallic Co peaks can be found at 44.2 and $51.5^{\circ}$ , which indicates that the composite obtained by carbonization is ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C@C C}}$ In addition, the main diffraction peaks of the sample produced by selenization at 34.2, 37.6, 43.7, and $51.8^{\circ}$ can be attributed to the (210), (211), (220), and (311) planes of $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ (JCPDS, no. 09-0234), respectively [39,40]. The XRD spectra reveal that the $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ catalysts have the same peak position as that of $\cos\mathbf{e}_{2}/\subset@\mathbf{CC}$ , but the intensity of some peaks is weakened, which demonstrates that P is doped into $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ . Subsequently, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was conducted to further analyze the surface chemical valence states of $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ and $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ Fig. S1b shows the highresolution Co 2p XPS spectrum of $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ revealing that the binding energies of Co $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ at 793.1 eV and $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ at $778.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponded to $C o^{2+}$ in ${\mathsf{C o S e}}_{2}$ . In addition, two apparent satellite peaks at $786.0\mathrm{eV}$ and $803.5\mathrm{eV}$ may originate from the antibonding orbital between Co and Se atoms, and two peaks are observed at $781.5\mathrm{eV}$ and 797.6 eV, most likely arising from the surface oxidation of Co 2p species [41,42]. Compared with $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ there is little change at the peak position of the high-resolution Co $\mathsf{2p}$ XPS spectrum of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ in Fig. 2b. The XPS spectrum of Se 3d for $\cos\mathbf{e}_{2}/\subset@\mathsf{C C}$ is illustrated in Fig. S1c, and the two peaks with electron binding energies at $53.9\ \mathrm{eV}$ and $54.7~\mathrm{eV}$ are probably due to the Se $3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}$ and Se $3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}$ electrons, respectively, corresponding to metal-selenium bonds. The other two peaks at $59.0\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and $60.8~\mathrm{eV}$ can be assigned to the formation of Se-O bonds, which is related to the oxidation of the surface Se elements [43,44]. As illustrated in Fig. 2c, the intensity of the oxidation peak is obviously weakened, appearing at the P-Se bond, which is formed due to phosphatization [45]. Fig. 2d represents the high-resolution $\textsf{P}2\mathsf{p}$ XPS spectrum, and the peaks at 128.9 and $129.7\mathrm{eV}$ can be ascribed to the P $2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ and P $2{\tt p}_{1/2}$ electrons, respectively. Similarly, the other peaks correspond to the $\mathtt{P-O}$ and P-Se bonds [46,47]. This result further confirms the successful incorporation of $\mathrm{~\bf~P~}$ and implies that P can alleviate the oxidation of Se by combining with oxidation, leading to improved stability of the material structure.
A typical scanning electron microscope (SEM) image of Co${\mathrm{MOF@CC}}$ is shown in Fig. 3a, and it exhibits a cuboid-like structure densely grown on carbon cloth. After carbonization, selenization and phosphorization, as shown in Figs. 3b–d and S2, the samples still maintain the framework of the precursor, suggesting the stability of the structure. Thermogravimetric (TG) analysis reveals that the decomposition temperature of Co-MOF is nearly $400~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ (see Fig. S3). The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) image of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ , illustrated in Fig. 3e, shows an obvious porous structure and a large number of rich active sites, which is advantageous to the diffusion of oxygen and electrolyte. Correspondingly, $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ shows a specific Brunauer-Emmett-Teller (BET) surface area of $41.849~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ and the mesoporous structure is centered at $30\ \mathrm{nm}$ , as shown in Fig. S4, suggesting that a high specific surface area and appropriate pore size are beneficial for its electrocatalytic performance. In the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image (Fig. 3f), $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ shows a carbon-encapsulated structure, which further ensures the stability of the composite materials. In addition, the HR-TEM image shows that the spacing values of the crystal lattice fringe are $0.262\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $0.258~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to the (210) lattice planes of $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{P-C o S e}_{2}$ , respectively (Fig. 3g–h). Also, the TEM-EDS mappings shown in Fig. 3i–l display the homogeneous distributions of the Co, Se, and P elements throughout $\mathrm{P-CoSe_{2}/C@C C}$
Fig. 4 compares the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) activity of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ and $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}/$ $C@C C$ . The electrocatalytic OER activity was first evaluated in $1.0\mathrm{M}$ KOH alkaline electrolyte with a scan rate of $2{\mathrm{~mV~}}s^{-1}$ using a traditional three-electrode system. As shown in Fig. 4a, the OER activity of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ is obviously better than that of ${\mathsf{C o S e}}_{2}/{\mathsf{C@C C}}$ It is revealed that the $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ catalyst entails a low overpotential of approximately $303.1\mathrm{mV}$ at a current density of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , which is superior to that of $\mathrm{CoSe_{2}/C@C C(338.6~m V)}$ , indicating that the incorporation of P further promotes the activity of the sample. The Tafel slope is also a critical standard to examine the catalytic kinetics of electrocatalysts. Fig. 4b demonstrates the Tafel slope of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ and $\cos\mathbf{e}_{2}/\subset@\mathbf{CC}$ . The Tafel slope of $\mathsf{P-C o S e}_{2}/$ ${\mathsf{C@C C}}$ is $71.3~\mathrm{mV}~\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ , which is slightly lower than that of $\mathsf{C o S e}_{2}/$ $\mathsf{C@C C}$ $72.3\ \mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1}$ ), suggesting that it has fast OER reaction kinetics and favorable catalytic activity. To further measure the electron transport kinetics, the fitted constants obtained from the electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) were explored in $1\mathrm{~M~}$ KOH at $1.57\mathrm{V}$ and compared to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), as shown in Table S1. Fig. 4c shows that the Faradaic resistance $\left(\mathsf{R}_{1}+\mathsf{R}_{2}\right.$ , denoted as $\mathsf{R}_{\mathrm{t}}$ ) of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ $(1.912\Omega)$ is significantly smaller than that of $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ (4.695 X), revealing its faster charge transport along with beneficial reaction kinetics [43]. In addition, plots of the current density at $1.05{\mathrm{~V~}}$ (V vs RHE) with different scan rates of $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ and $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ are recorded in Fig. 4d, indicating the electrochemical double-layer capacitance $\left(\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}\right)$ . $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ possesses a larger $\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{dl}}$ $22.5~\mathrm{mF}$ $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) value than $\mathrm{CoSe_{2}/C@C C}(20.9\mathrm{mFcm^{-2}})$ , which demonstrates that a much larger electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) improves the OER activity. In conclusion, the P-doped catalyst exhibits a better OER electrocatalytic performance. The long-term stability is also a critical factor to evaluate the electrocatalytic property of the catalyst. Fig. 4f shows the chronopotentiometry curve of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ at a current density of $20\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . There is no distinct fluctuation after running for 10000 s. Moreover, as illustrated in Fig. 4f, the LSV and CV curves after 500 and 2000 CV cycles have a slight change compared to the initial cycle. In particular, even after 2000 cycles, the overpotential of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ $(386\mathrm{mV})$ is still lower than that of $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ ( $400\mathrm{mV})$ at a current density of $50\ m A\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ . Thus, $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ exhibits good durability during OER catalysis. The XRD and TEM of $\mathsf{P-C o S e}_{2}/$ ${\mathsf{C@C C}}$ after OER are investigated in Fig. S5, which reveal that the original $\mathsf{P-C o S e}_{2}$ -based crystal phase has converted into an amorphous structure during the reaction. The generated amorphous phase may be due to cobalt oxide/hydroxide or $\mathsf{S e O}_{\mathrm{x}}$ , which is the active structure responsible for the OER reaction [46].
The ORR activity of the samples is detected in 0.1 M KOH in an $0_{2}$ -saturated state. The polarization curves before and after P doping are analyzed in Fig. $4g$ , which shows a similar reduction peak. Furthermore, compared to the initial cycle, there is an obvious reduction peak of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ after $500\mathrm{CV}$ cycles and no significant change after 1000 CV cycles, which may be due to the stable activation during this operation (Fig. 4h). As expected, Fig. 4i shows that the P-doped material also displays remarkable stability in a typical solution within $10000\ s$ Thus, the above results illustrate that $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ exhibits enhanced electrocatalytic activity and considerable durability in the ORR.
In addition, the contact angles of the bare CC, Co-MOF@CC, Co/ $\mathsf{C@C C}$ , $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ , and $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ are compared in Fig. S6, which is also an indispensable factor affecting the electrocatalytic properties of catalysts. The contact angles of the bare CC, Co${\mathsf{M O F@C C}}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}/{\mathsf{C@C C}}$ and $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ are approximately $120.41^{\circ}$ , $96.97^{\circ}$ , $60.20^{\circ}$ , and $18.9^{\circ}$ , respectively, after diffusion for $3\ s$ exhibiting that the wettability of the materials improves with the progress of different stages. Unexpectedly, when a water droplet is dropped on the surface of $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ , it adsorbs rapidly, indicating an excellent superhydrophilic feature. Thus, $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ possesses the best wettability with the electrolyte, which may depend on the porous structure and intrinsic nature of the materials, producing many active sites to accelerate the catalytic reaction rate.
Since the $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ catalyst showed better performance than $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ , we explored the potential application of the P$\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ catalyst in rechargeable zinc-air batteries. A homemade device was assembled with $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ as the cathode and other components (see Fig. S7). The zinc-air battery with the $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ air–cathode exhibits a high open circuit potential of ${\approx}1.455\ \mathrm{V},$ as shown in Fig. 5a, which is larger than the ${\mathsf{C o S e}}_{2}/$ ${\mathsf{C@C C}}$ air–cathode (see Fig. S8a). The discharge polarization curves of the Zn-air battery and the corresponding power density are shown in Fig. 5b. The peak power density of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -air battery with the $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ catalyst is as high as $124.4\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ , which is much better than that of $\mathsf{C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ and some reported metal selenide-based materials on Zn-air batteries (see Figs. 5c and S8b and Table S2). The cycle stability is one of the most crucial factors for the performance of zinc-air batteries. As clearly depicted in
Fig. 5d, the discharge–charge curve of the Zn-air battery can remain stable at current densities different from 1, 2, 5, 7.5 to $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . The stability of the Zn-air battery is further completed by cycling discharge–charge tests at $10\mathrm{min}$ per cycle, as shown in Fig. 5e (charge and discharge for 5 min separately). Fig. 5e clearly shows that the charge–discharge voltage of the battery does not decay much after cycling for nearly $70\mathrm{h}$ without replacing the zinc flake. In particular, the initial round-trip efficiency of the Zn-air battery with $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ is approximately $68.7\%$ , and still maintains an approximate value of $68.8\ \%$ over $^{70\mathrm{~h~}}$ . These results further confirm that the bifunctional $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ is feasible in practical applications with augmented cyclic stability.
## 4. Conclusions
In summary, phosphorus-doped transition metal selenide/carbon composite catalysts derived from metal-organic frameworks $\left(\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}}/\mathsf{C@C C}\right)$ were constructed by a self-supporting carbon cloth structure through a simple solvothermal method with a subsequent selenization and phosphatization process. Owing to the specific architecture, the superhydrophilic surface and the synergistic effect of P-doping, $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ demonstrates outstanding catalytic activity and stability toward both the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) and the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR). More importantly, $\mathsf{P-C o S e_{2}/C@C C}$ exhibits a remarkable power density of $124.4\mathrm{\mW\cm^{-2}}$ compared to other metal selenide-based materials [9,15,32,43,48–49] and superior cycling stability without significant decay after $70\mathrm{h}$ when it was established as the air cathode in Zn-air batteries. Thus, this work provides a new strategy for the rational design of modified OER/ORR bifunctional electrocatalysts by heteroatom-doped transition metal selenides for metal-air batteries.
